thout reading the code.CHANGES (create helix_codex_app/governance.md with these sections, in this order):1. Title, and one line: what this file is for.2. Subordination. Name the authority chain in one sentence: 00_CONSTITUTION.md, then   docs/HELIX_CODEX_OS_MASTER_BLUEPRINT.md, then this file. Say plainly that on any conflict the   constitution wins.3. Scope. What the app is, and what it is not. Include the deferred list from the master plan   section 14 so nobody re-litigates it.4. Inherited invariants, by reference rather than by restatement. Name each one and point at the   parent rule: tenant, client, classification, provenance, and correlation context survive every   boundary; sensitive actions fail closed; simulated, historical, and live data stay visibly   distinct; self-improvement is a proposal until isolated evaluation, review, approval, versioning,   and rollback exist.5. App-specific rules. At minimum: sessions are opaque cookies with hashed tokens and CSRF   double-submit; cockpit.view is enforced in three places (router dependency, service check, policy   bridge); the app-local role layer in security/permissions.py never edits the parent role catalog;   helix_codex_app/integration/ is the only parent-importing package; record_node() is the only   writer for governed events.6. The honest statement about per-user memory. Write it in plain words: each account has its own   governed memory store, the user proposes and a human approves, nothing applies by itself, and   managers can see the org store and the promotion queue. Say clearly what it does not mean — it is   not a private place to hide, and every record still carries tenant, classification, provenance,   and evidence. This paragraph exists because over-claiming would break the first principle of the   constitution.7. Decision log. Start it with the eight locked decisions from the master plan section 2, each dated   2026-09-13, and each stating the assumption behind it.CONSTRAINTS:- Plain human English. No filler, no marketing adjectives, no rule-of-three padding.- Do not duplicate the constitution's text. Reference it.- Every rule must be checkable. If a rule cannot be tested, rewrite it until it can, or drop it.VERIFY:- Read the file back and confirm every section 1-7 is present.- Confirm no rule restates a constitution rule verbatim.- Confirm the decision log has eight dated entries.COMMIT: docs(app): add app governance rules and decision log```---Prompt 4 — P0.4: Write agents.md and repomap.md```PREAMBLEGOAL: Create the app's build ledger and its map. These two files are what let a fresh agent resumewithout re-deriving anything.CONTEXT: The parent AGENTS.md is the ledger for the ops core. It uses a status table, a step ledgerwith checkboxes, non-negotiable rules, and a git protocol. The app's agents.md mirrors that formatexactly, so one habit works in both places. The parent has no repomap.md; docs/architecture/REPO_GRAPH.md is the closest thing, and it describes the core, not the app.CHANGES:1. Create helix_codex_app/agents.md with:   - Project context: what the app is, in three sentences, and that its plan lives at     docs/HELIX_CODEX_APP_MASTER_PLAN.md.   - Authority chain, one line.   - Non-negotiables. Inherit the parent AGENTS.md section 0 by reference, then add the app-specific     ones: never edit the parent catalog files, never bypass record_node(), never add a JS build step,     never commit app.db or memory_stores/, integration/ is the only parent-importing package.   - A status table with rows: Current step, Baseline test count (621), Last commit, Completed steps.   - A step ledger with checkboxes for P0 through P7, each step listing its prompts.   - Git protocol: feat(app):, test(app):, fix(app):, docs:, chore(app):; never git add -A; never     commit .db, __pycache__, .venv*, memory_stores/, or evidence/.   - The check-in / check-out protocol, copied from this prompt pack secti