 tenant_id, a blank correlation_id, an unknown   classification, a missing nature, a missing provenance data_mode, and a blank created_by, by   raising. Add close() so tests can release the handle on Windows.2. helix_codex_app/alembic.ini — script_location = helix_codex_app/migrations, sqlalchemy.url left   blank (the path comes from -x db= or HELIX_APP_DB_PATH).3. helix_codex_app/migrations/env.py, script.py.mako, versions/0001_codex_app_baseline.py — the   baseline migration creating the same schema as _init_schema(), using raw op.execute DDL to match   the parent style. There are no ORM models in this repo; do not introduce any.4. helix_codex_app/scripts/check_app_migration_drift.py — builds one database from db.py and one from   alembic upgrade head, then compares sqlite_master. Exit non-zero on any difference. Mirror   scripts/check_migration_drift.py in shape.CONSTRAINTS:- No ORM. Raw sqlite3 and raw DDL, exactly like the parent.- Keep the SQL ANSI-ish and avoid SQLite-only syntax in new tables, so a later Postgres move is a  connection change and not a rewrite.- record_node() is the only function that may INSERT into nodes. Nothing else may.VERIFY:- .venv-py312\Scripts\python.exe helix_codex_app/scripts/check_app_migration_drift.py exits 0.- A unit test asserts record_node() raises on a blank tenant_id, a blank correlation_id, and an  unknown classification.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean on helix_codex_app/.COMMIT: feat(app): add app database, schema bootstrap, and governed node writer```---Prompt 6 — P1.2: Accounts, domains, and passwords```PREAMBLEGOAL: Real user accounts with domain-scoped usernames and safely stored passwords.CONTEXT: Today the only auth in the repo is a single shared bearer token in server/auth.py. That isfine for headless ops and must not change. The app needs per-person accounts. One domain is onetenant: a login is username@domain and the domain supplies tenant_id and client_id. Passwords usehashlib.scrypt from the standard library, so no new dependency and no hand-written cryptography.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/security/passwords.py — hash_password() producing   "scrypt$n$r$p$salt_b64$hash_b64" with a 16-byte salt, n=2**14, r=8, p=1; verify_password() using   hmac.compare_digest; needs_rehash() that returns True when stored parameters are below current   policy. Fail closed: an unparseable stored hash returns False from verify, never True.2. helix_codex_app/security/accounts.py — dataclasses Domain, OrgUnit, Account plus a repository   with: create_domain, get_domain_by_name, create_org_unit, list_org_units, create_account,   get_account_by_login(domain_name, username), get_account_by_id, list_accounts(domain_id),   update_account, set_status, record_failed_attempt, reset_failed_attempts, lock_account.   Usernames are stored twice: as typed, and normalised to lowercase in username_normalized, which   carries the unique constraint.3. helix_codex_app/security/limits.py — default limits per app role (employee: storage_mb 500,   messages_per_day 2000; manager higher; owner unlimited), seed_limits_for_account(), and   check_and_consume(account, key, amount) which raises LimitExceeded when a quota is spent.4. Tests in tests/helix_codex_app/test_passwords.py and test_accounts.py: a correct password verifies,   a wrong one does not, a tampered hash does not, needs_rehash() is True for a weaker stored cost,   and a duplicate username within one domain is rejected while the same username in another domain   is allowed.CONSTRAINTS:- Standard library only for hashing. Do not add argon2, bcrypt, or passlib.- Never log a password or a hash. Never return a hash in an API response.- Every repository read that returns an account must take tenant scope from the domain, not from a  caller-supplied tenant_id.VERIFY:- The two new test modules pass.- Full suite >= 621 + the new tests passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean on helix_codex_app/ and tests/helix_codex_app/.COMMIT: feat(app): add domain-scoped accounts and scrypt password storage```---Prompt 7 — P1.3: Sessions, cookies, and CSRF```PREAMBLEGOAL: Session handling and the request guard, so that every /app route knows who is calling.CONTEXT: Sessions are opaque, not JWTs. One lookup table is simpler to reason about and — moreimportantly — can be revoked, which a manager disabling an account requires. Only the hash of thesession token is stored, so a database leak does not hand over live sessions.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/security/sessions.py — issue_session(account, ip, user_agent) returning an opaque   token from secrets.token_urlsafe(32), storing only its SHA-256 hash and a CSRF token; verify()   that rejects missing, expired, revoked, and locked sessions; revoke(session_id); revoke_all_for   (account_id); touch(session_id) to refresh last_seen_at. Idle timeout from   settings.session_idle_minutes, absolute from settings.session_absolute_days.2. helix_codex_app/security/guard.py — FastAPI dependencies: current_account (reads the helix_session   cookie, verifies the session, loads the account, raises AuthError on failure);   require_permission(key); require_capability(key); require_csrf (compares the X-CSRF-Token header   against the session's token with compare_digest, raises on mismatch); require_scope(tenant_id)   which raises when the requested scope is not the caller's own.3. helix_codex_app/app.py — include a router-level dependency so that every non-GET /app/* route runs   require_csrf, and every /app/* route except the health route and the static mount runs   current_account. Do this once, at the router boundary, not per handler.4. Set the cookie HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, with a path of /. Make Secure conditional on a   settings flag so local development over plain HTTP still works, and default that flag to True.CONSTRAINTS:- No JWT. No itsdangerous. No signing library.- The CSRF check must be a router-level dependency, not something each handler remembers to call.- A revoked session must fail on the very next request.VERIFY:- Tests: a request with no cookie gets 401; a request with a valid cookie gets 200; a non-GET without  the CSRF header gets 403; a non-GET with the wrong CSRF token gets 403; a revoked session gets 401;  an expired session gets 401.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add opaque cookie sessions and csrf guard```---Prompt 8 — P1.4: The permission catalog and role mapping```PREAMBLEGOAL: The app's own role and permission layer, and the bridge that maps an account to the corepolicy engine.CONTEXT: organization/role-catalog.yaml holds nine GM and executive roles and must never be edited.Ordinary employees will not have one. So the app defines five of its own roles — owner, manager,employee, contractor, external — and maps them to permissions. When an app account reaches the corepolicy engine, its role_id is either one of the nine catalog ids or None. An app role with no catalogequivalent gets None, and the core then denies by construction. That is how an employee is kept outof ops: not by a filter, but because the policy engine has never heard of them.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/security/permissions.py — the permission catalog and the role-to-permission map   exactly as in the master plan section 5.5. Add permissions_for(account) and has_permission   (account, key). Add a constant listing the nine catalog role ids that count as privileged, read   from organization/role_catalog.py at import time.2. helix_codex_app/integration/policy_bridge.py — to_identity(account) building a   security.identity.Identity with actor=account_id, actor_type="human", the account's tenant_id and   client_id, and role_id set only when the app role maps to a catalog role. Add   authorize_engine_action(account, request) that calls security.policy.authorize and raises   PermissionDenied on a deny. Never default to allow.3. Tests: has_permission for every role and permission pair in the matrix; to_identity() returns   role_id None for an employee; authorize_engine_action raises for an employee and passes for an   owner.CONSTRAINTS:- Do not edit organization/role-catalog.yaml or control_plane/governance.py. Read only.- policy_bridge.py lives in integration/ — it is the only place allowed to import security.policy.- Deny by default. An unknown permission key returns False, never True.VERIFY:- The matrix test covers all 13 permissions across all 5 app roles plus the catalog roles.- An employee identity reaching authorize_engine_action gets a PermissionDenied, proven by test.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add app permission catalog and core policy bridge```---Prompt 9 — P1.5: Login, logout, and the auth screens```PREAMBLEGOAL: A person can sign in with username@domain and a password, and sign out.CONTEXT: The login screen is the first thing every user sees, so it has to be clean and it has towork one-handed on a phone. Login must be rate-limited by lockout: repeated failures lock theaccount for a period, and the lockout is recorded.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/identity/service.py — login(domain_name, username, password, ip,   user_agent): resolves the domain, then the account, verifies the password, applies lockout rules   (lock after 5 consecutive failures for 15 minutes), resets the failure count on success, writes a   login_events row for every outcome, and issues a session. logout(session_id). change_password   (account, old, new) which clears must_change_password.2. helix_codex_app/modules/identity/router.py — POST /app/auth/login (form), POST /app/auth/logout,   GET /app/auth/me, POST /app/auth/password. Every response is an HTMX-friendly fragment or a   redirect; no JSON-only endpoints for the browser.3. helix_codex_app/templates/auth/login.html — domain field, username field, password field, one   primary button. Inline error text under the field that failed. Large tap targets. No decorative   imagery. It must be obvious what to do.4. helix_codex_app/templates/shell/home.html — greet the signed-in user by display name and show   their role.5. Tests: successful login sets a cookie; wrong password returns a form error and increments the   counter; the sixth failure locks the account and the lock is recorded; a locked account cannot log   in even with the right password; logout revokes the session; a user with must_change_password is   redirected to the password screen.CONSTRAINTS:- Never reveal whether the domain or the username was the wrong part. One message: the sign-in  details did not match.- The lockout counter and the login_events row are written even when the domain does not exist.- Plain, short UI copy. "Sign in", not "Authenticate to your workspace".VERIFY:- All login tests pass.- Load /app/auth/login at 375px and confirm it is usable without zooming.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add domain login, lockout, and auth screens```---Prompt 10 — P1.6: Admin — users, domains, org units, capabilities, limits```PREAMBLEGOAL: An owner or manager can create and manage accounts, domains, and org units, and can grant orrevoke capabilities and adjust limits.CONTEXT: This is the screen that makes the app usable by a real company on day one. It isowner-and-manager only. A manager may manage accounts inside their own org unit; an owner may managethe whole domain.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/admin/service.py — create_user, update_user, set_user_status,   grant_capability, revoke_capability, set_limit, create_domain, create_org_unit, list_users. Every   write goes through record_node(). A manager acting outside their org unit raises PermissionDenied.2. helix_codex_app/modules/admin/router.py — GET /app/admin, GET/POST /app/admin/users,   PATCH /app/admin/users/{id}, POST /app/admin/users/{id}/capabilities,   POST /app/admin/users/{id}/limits, GET/POST /app/admin/domains, GET/POST /app/admin/org-units.   The router is included with require_permission("admin.users").3. Templates: helix_codex_app/templates/admin/users.html (list with search and a create form),   user_detail.html (status, role, capabilities, limits), domains.html, org_units.html. Tables on   desktop, cards on mobile.4. Tests: an owner can create a user in their domain; a manager can create a user only in their own   org unit and gets 403 otherwise; an employee gets 403 on every admin route; granting a capability   is reflected in has_permission immediately; setting a limit below current usage does not crash.CONSTRAINTS:- Admin is owner-and-manager only, enforced at the router boundary and re-checked in the service.- Never allow an account to change its own role or grant itself a capability.- Every admin action lands in the audit trail through record_node().VERIFY:- All admin tests pass, including the employee 403 on every route.- Create a user through the UI and confirm they can sign in.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add admin screens for users, domains, and limits```---Prompt 11 — P1.7: Close out P1 — isolation, PWA assets, and the ledger```PREAMBLEGOAL: Prove P1 is actually safe, then close the phase.CONTEXT: A phase is not done when the features work. It is done when the boundaries are tested andthe ledger says so. Tenant isolation and cockpit exclusion are the two boundaries that matter most,and they must have tests before any more features are built on top.CHANGES:1. tests/helix_codex_app/test_tenant_isolation.py — for each table that carries tenant_id, assert   that a query issued by an account in tenant A never returns a row from tenant B. Cover accounts,   domains, org units, and sessions at minimum.2. tests/helix_codex_app/test_cockpit_exclusion.py — an employee session gets 403 on every cockpit   route that exists so far. If no cockpit route exists yet, write the test against the permission   check directly and mark it to be extended in P6.3. tests/helix_codex_app/test_pwa_assets.py — manifest.webmanifest parses and lists three icons; each   icon file exists; sw.js exists and references a versioned cache name; offline.html exists.4. tests/helix_codex_app/test_migration_drift.py — runs check_app_migration_drift.py and asserts   exit 0.5. Update helix_codex_app/agents.md — tick P1.1 through P1.7, record the new test count and the SHA.6. Update helix_codex_app/repomap.md with the modules that now exist.CONSTRAINTS:- These are tests, plus ledger updates. Do not add features in this prompt.- If a test exposes a real isolation bug, fix the bug in the same commit and say so in the report.VERIFY:- The four new test modules pass.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean on helix_codex_app/ and tests/helix_codex_app/.COMMIT: test(app): prove tenant isolation and close phase p1```---6. PHASE P2 — Messaging, notifications, SSEPrompt 12 — P2.1: Conversations and messages```PREAMBLEGOAL: Direct and group conversations, with messages stored and readable.CONTEXT: Chat is the most-used surface in the app, so the data model has to be right before the UIis built. Conversations are tenant-scoped. Membership is explicit. A message is both a working-tablerow and a governed node, so it lands in the audit trail like everything else.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/messaging/repository.py — create_conversation, add_member,   remove_member, list_conversations(account), get_conversation(conversation_id, account) which   raises NotFoundError when the account is not a member, append_message, list_messages   (conversation_id, before, limit), mark_read.2. helix_codex_app/modules/messaging/service.py — create_direct(a, b) which reuses an existing direct   conversation between the same two accounts, create_group(name, members), send_message(account,   conversation_id, body) which writes the row and then calls record_node() with kind message,   classification internal, and the conversation's correlation_id.3. helix_codex_app/modules/messaging/schemas.py — request and response models with pydantic.4. Tests: a non-member cannot read or post; create_direct called twice returns the same conversation;   sending a message creates exactly one nodes row; list_messages paginates correctly.CONSTRAINTS:- Membership is the only access rule. Tenant scope comes from the account, never from the request.- No message editing or deletion yet; the columns exist but the endpoints come later.VERIFY:- The messaging tests pass.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add conversations and messages```---Prompt 13 — P2.2: The chat UI and the SSE stream```PREAMBLEGOAL: A working chat screen that updates live, and the SSE endpoint behind it.CONTEXT: The parent already has an SSE seam at server/sse.py::EventBus. Reuse it. The screen has tobe excellent on a phone: a conversation list, a thread, and a composer that stays above the keyboard.Sending must feel instant, so the message is appended optimistically and reconciled when the serverconfirms.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/messaging/router.py — GET /app/chat, GET /app/chat/{conversation_id},   POST /app/api/conversations, GET /app/api/conversations,   GET/POST /app/api/conversations/{id}/messages, POST /app/api/conversations/{id}/read,   GET /app/api/conversations/{id}/stream (SSE).2. helix_codex_app/integration/ — add the SSE bridge if the EventBus needs a thin adapter; keep the   import inside integration/.3. helix_codex_app/static/js/sse.js — an EventSource wrapper that reconnects with backoff and   dispatches a custom event HTMX can listen for.4. Templates: partials/chat_list.html, partials/chat_thread.html, partials/composer.html. The thread   appends new messages, scrolls to the newest,