orage. Both are small, contained fixes,and both need a test that fails before the fix and passes after. Fixing them here, in the parent, iscorrect: the app reuses this engine verbatim, so a bug in the engine is a bug in every user's memory.CHANGES (metacognition/improvement.py and its tests only):1. Tighten the approvable set to evaluated proposals only. An approve() on anything else must raise a   typed error, not return a refusal that a caller might ignore.2. Make the correct, supersede, and delete flags survive a save and reload round trip.3. Tests in tests/test_metacognition.py: approving an unevaluated proposal raises; each flag survives   a round trip; the existing behaviour is otherwise unchanged.CONSTRAINTS:- Minimal diff. Do not restructure the module.- Do not change the public function signatures.- Keep the existing separation-of-duties behaviour exactly as it is.VERIFY:- The new tests fail on the pre-fix code and pass after it. Show both runs in the report.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean on metacognition/ and tests/.COMMIT: fix(gov): tighten approvable proposals and preserve correction flags```---Prompt 25 — P5.2: The per-account memory store```PREAMBLEGOAL: Give every account its own governed memory store, and one org store per tenant.CONTEXT: This is the distinctive promise of the app: each user's memory improves with use, underhuman approval, and it is theirs. Isolation is by construction — a store is resolved from theauthenticated account id, never from a request parameter. There is also one org store per tenant forknowledge the whole company shares, curated by managers and owners.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/integration/memory_bridge.py — AccountMemoryStore wrapping   memory/governed_memory.py::GovernedMemory. It resolves a store path from   settings.memory_root/<tenant_id>/<account_id>/. Provide record(account, kind, nature, body,   evidence_refs), read(account, limit), verify_chain(account), and the org equivalents keyed by   _org instead of an account id. Add resolve_store(account) as the single place a path is computed.   Never accept an account id as a lookup key from a caller that is not the authenticated account.2. helix_codex_app/db.py — implement the memory_stores table usage: register_store(store_id,   tenant_id, account_id, kind, path), touch_store(...) to keep record_count and chain_head current,   list_stores(tenant_id).3. helix_codex_app/deps.py — a provider that hands out an AccountMemoryStore bound to the current   account.4. Tests: two accounts in the same tenant get different store paths; a record written by A never   appears in B's store; the org store is shared; verify_chain passes on a fresh store; a tampered   line fails verification.CONSTRAINTS:- memory_bridge.py lives in integration/. It is the only file that imports memory/.- A store path is never derived from anything the client sends.- Keep GovernedMemory.auto_apply_policies disabled. Memory never silently changes behaviour.VERIFY:- The isolation tests pass and would fail if resolve_store() used a request value.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add per-account governed memory stores```---Prompt 26 — P5.3: Proposals, reviews, and the projection tables```PREAMBLEGOAL: The proposal lifecycle in the app's own tables, backed by the hash-chained ledger.CONTEXT: The JSONL ledger from memory/governed_memory.py stays the source of truth. The proposals andproposal_reviews tables are a projection so the UI can list and filter quickly. If the two everdisagree, the ledger wins and the projection is rebuilt.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/memory/repository.py and service.py — propose(account, kind, target,   payload, evidence_refs), evaluate(proposal_id) which runs the metacognition evaluation and stores   the result, approve(proposal_id, reviewer), reject(proposal_id, reviewer, reason),   rollback(proposal_id, actor), list_proposals(account, filters), get_proposal(proposal_id).   Separation of duties is delegated to the engine and surfaced as a typed refusal.2. helix_codex_app/integration/metacognition_bridge.py — propose, evaluate, approve, reject,   rollback, generate_evidence_report, verify_chain. One engine instance per store.3. helix_codex_app/modules/memory/router.py — GET /app/memory, GET /app/memory/proposals,   GET /app/memory/proposals/{id}, POST /app/api/memory/proposals,   POST /app/api/memory/proposals/{id}/evaluate, /approve, /reject, /rollback,   GET /app/memory/ledger/verify.4. Tests: a proposal can be created and evaluated; approving before evaluation raises; a reviewer   cannot approve their own proposal; rollback restores the prior state and records it; a rebuilt   projection matches the ledger.CONSTRAINTS:- The ledger is the source of truth. A projection write never precedes the ledger write.- Every state change is recorded in the account's store and in the nodes table.VERIFY:- The lifecycle tests pass, including the separation-of-duties denial.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add memory proposals, reviews, and rollback```---Prompt 27 — P5.4: The memory screen```PREAMBLEGOAL: A screen where a user sees their memory, reads a proposal's evidence, and decides.CONTEXT: The whole promise rests on this screen being honest. A reviewer must see why a proposal isgood or bad — the baseline rate, the candidate rate, the difference, how many historical andsimulated cases were used — not a green tick. Approve, reject, and rollback are the only actions, andeach says what it will do.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/templates/memory.html — the account's memory: recent records, and the list of   proposals with their state.2. helix_codex_app/templates/partials/proposal_card.html — a state chip, the evidence table, the risk   note, the rollback plan, and the chain status.3. helix_codex_app/templates/partials/proposal_actions.html — approve, reject, rollback as HTMX form   posts. Reject requires a reason. Rollback is shown only when the proposal is applied.4. helix_codex_app/templates/partials/data_mode_badge.html — the simulated-data badge, shown on every   screen that displays simulated numbers.5. Copy rules: the approve button says what changes. The empty state explains what a proposal is in   one sentence. No jargon a new employee would not know.6. Tests: the evidence table renders the four numbers; the rollback control is absent until the   proposal is applied; the page shows only the account's own store.CONSTRAINTS:- No dark patterns. No default-to-approve. No countdown.- The simulated-data badge is never hidden. It is a constitution requirement.VERIFY:- Walk the full path in a browser: create, evaluate, read the evidence, approve, roll back.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add the memory screen and proposal review ui```---Prompt 28 — P5.5: Promotion into org memory```PREAMBLEGOAL: A proposal that proves out for one user can be promoted into the org store, with a secondindependent approver.CONTEXT: Isolation is useful only if good ideas can travel. Promotion is the bridge: a user'sapproved improvement can be proposed for everyone, but the user's own approval is never enough. Amanager or owner must approve the promotion. That is the whole point — the individual improves theirown work, the organization adopts what works, and nobody can promote their own rule company-wide.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/memory/service.py — request_promotion(account, proposal_id) which creates   a proposal in the org store that references the source proposal and its evidence; approve_promotion   (promotion_id, approver) which requires the approver to be a manager or owner and to differ from   the requesting account; rollback_promotion(promotion_id, actor) which removes the org rule and   records the reversal in both stores.2. helix_codex_app/modules/memory/router.py — POST /app/api/memory/promotions,   GET /app/memory/promotions (manager and owner only),   POST /app/api/memory/promotions/{id}/approve, /reject, /rollback.3. helix_codex_app/templates/promotions.html — the promotion review queue for managers.4. Tests: a user cannot approve their own promotion; a manager can; the org store gains exactly one   rule on approval; rolling back removes it from the org store and records the reversal in both   stores; an employee gets 403 on the promotion queue.CONSTRAINTS:- The second-approver rule is enforced in the service, not only in the UI.- Provenance on the org rule records promoted_from_account_id, source_proposal_id, and evidence_refs.VERIFY:- The promotion tests pass, including the self-approval denial.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add governed promotion into org memory```---Prompt 29 — P5.6: Close out P5```PREAMBLEGOAL: Prove P5 and close the phase.CHANGES:1. tests/helix_codex_app/test_memory_store_isolation.py — A's store never contains B's records, for   both records and proposals, across the read, verify, and rollback paths.2. tests/helix_codex_app/test_proposal_lifecycle.py — the full path, including the separation-of-duty   denial and the rollback, end to end.3. tests/helix_codex_app/test_promotion_second_approver.py — self-approval denied, manager approval   accepted, employee denied.4. tests/helix_codex_app/test_ledger_verify.py — a tampered line fails verification; an intact chain   passes.5. Update helix_codex_app/agents.md, governance.md (the honest memory paragraph, with a pointer to   these tests), and repomap.md.CONSTRAINTS:- Tests and document updates only.VERIFY:- The four new test modules pass.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: test(app): prove memory isolation and approval rules, close phase p5```---10. PHASE P6 — Operations and the cockpitPrompt 30 — P6.1: The engine bridge, done properly```PREAMBLEGOAL: A complete engine bridge, so the ops section and the cockpit both read the core through oneplace.CONTEXT: engines/ and control_plane/ are the core. The app talks to them only throughhelix_codex_app/integration/. The bridge must never silently degrade: if the engine cannot be read,it raises. An operations screen showing stale numbers as if they were live is worse than an error.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/integration/engine_bridge.py — wrap control_plane/engine.py::Engine. Provide   submit_workflow(account, request), get_workflow(workflow_id), list_workflows(account, filters),   list_approvals(account), approve(workflow_id, account, decision), engine_status(engine_id), and   list_engines(). Every call builds an Identity through policy_bridge and calls authorize first.2. helix_codex_app/integration/packs.py — discover capability packs under capabilities/ by reading   each pack's register.py metadata, and expose list_packs() and pack_sections(pack).3. Tests: a call without permission raises PermissionDenied; an engine that raises propagates; the   bridge never returns a fabricated empty result when the engine is down.CONSTRAINTS:- This file is the only place that imports engines/ and control_plane/.- No HTTP self-calls. Everything is in-process.- Fail closed on every path.VERIFY:- The bridge tests pass.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add engine bridge for ops and cockpit```---Prompt 31 — P6.2: The Operations section```PREAMBLEGOAL: An Operations section for managers and owners: engine overview, and governed workflowsubmission with an approval step.CONTEXT: This is where Helix Prime's engine becomes something an employee can actually use. Amanager submits a request, the governance rail routes it, and an approver decides. The screen mustshow the state of each request plainly: submitted, awaiting approval, approved, closed, refused