 switch state, and recent audit activity. It is read-only.CHANGES:1. Extend helix_codex_app/integration/cockpit_bridge.py for the coach and parent views.2. helix_codex_app/modules/cockpit/router.py — wire the remaining routes.3. Templates: cockpit_coach.html, cockpit_parent.html, cockpit_control_plane.html. Engine status   rows, kill switch state, and the most recent audit entries with their correlation ids.4. Tests: each new view requires cockpit.view; the control plane panel never exposes a write action;   the audit entries shown match the core audit store.CONSTRAINTS:- Read-only. The control plane panel shows state; it does not change it. If a write action is needed  later, it goes through the ops section and its approval rail, not here.- Do not retire the Streamlit app in this prompt. It stays for one release, as the blueprint planned.VERIFY:- The new cockpit tests pass.- Load all cockpit routes as an owner and as an employee; confirm 200 and 403 respectively.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add coach, parent, and control plane cockpit views```---Prompt 34 — P6.5: Close out P6```PREAMBLEGOAL: Prove P6 and close the phase.CHANGES:1. tests/helix_codex_app/test_cockpit_requires_permission.py — every cockpit route returns 403 for an   employee, a contractor, and an external account, and 200 for a manager and an owner.2. tests/helix_codex_app/test_cockpit_cross_tenant.py — an owner in tenant A cannot read tenant B's   cockpit numbers.3. tests/helix_codex_app/test_ops_lifecycle.py — submit, approve, refuse, close, with the correlation   id intact throughout.4. Update helix_codex_app/agents.md, governance.md, and repomap.md.CONSTRAINTS:- Tests and document updates only.VERIFY:- The three new test modules pass.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: test(app): prove cockpit gating and ops lifecycle, close phase p6```---11. PHASE P7 — Low-code, release, packaging, signoffPrompt 35 — P7.1: The low-code capability loader```PREAMBLEGOAL: Load capability packs from a manifest, enforce the five invariants, and let a pack contributesections to the shell.CONTEXT: The blueprint section 2.3 defines this: a capability.yaml per pack and a CapabilityPackProtocol. The point is that a client or a third party can add a section without touching core code,and the five invariants stop a pack from quietly widening its own authority. Sections are data: theshell renders whatever is registered and permitted.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/lowcode/pack_loader.py — load_pack(path), validate_pack(pack),   register_pack(pack). Implement the CapabilityPack Protocol with manifest, ontology, roles,   workflows, metrics, runtime.2. Enforce the five invariants, each raising a typed error: a pack role cannot widen a core role's   max_financial_amount; a pack cannot register a capability another pack owns; a pack with   production_readiness other than ESTABLISHED cannot declare a live data_mode; a min_core_version   above the runtime refuses to load; a pack role cannot review its own actions.3. Write capabilities/sports_academy/capability.yaml per the master plan section 10. Do not modify   the pack's existing Python.4. helix_codex_app/modules/lowcode/section_registry.py — register the sections a pack declares into   the sections table, and have the shell render nav entries from the table, filtered by the   account's permissions.5. helix_codex_app/modules/lowcode/router.py — GET /app/api/sections, POST /app/admin/sections,   GET /app/api/packs, POST /app/admin/packs/reload (owner only).6. Tests: one test per invariant, each proving the loader raises; a section with a   required_capability is hidden from an account without it, and its route returns 403.CONSTRAINTS:- A pack cannot add code to core. It declares; the core loads.- The section route gate is the same require_permission mechanism as everywhere else.VERIFY:- The invariant tests pass, one per invariant.- Add a second throwaway pack manifest in the test fixtures and confirm it loads and registers a  section.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add capability pack loader with enforced invariants```---Prompt 36 — P7.2: App release gates```PREAMBLEGOAL: Give the app its own release gates, without weakening the core's.CONTEXT: release/gate.py runs gates by profile and emits only CONTROLLED_PILOT_READY orPRODUCTION_CANDIDATE. The bare PRODUCTION label is unreachable by construction, and that must staytrue. The app adds its own gates and its own profile, and it must not touch the core's gate list.CHANGES:1. release/gate.py — add app gate functions with the same (ok, detail) contract:   app_auth_boundary (no unauthenticated /app route except health and static),   app_session_fail_closed (a revoked and an expired session both fail),   app_tenant_isolation (the isolation tests pass), app_memory_store_isolation (the store tests pass),   app_migration_drift (check_app_migration_drift.py exits 0), app_pwa_assets (manifest, service   worker, offline page, and icons all present and valid).2. release/release-profiles.yaml — add an app_pilot profile requiring repository_state,   configuration_validation, startup_readiness, data_isolation, audit_integrity, plus the six new   app gates. Do not modify the existing profiles or the production gate list.3. Tests: the app_pilot profile runs and reports; each new gate fails when its condition is broken,   proven by a temporary fixture.CONSTRAINTS:- Do not weaken or reorder the existing 14 gates or the 9 production gates.- The allowed final labels stay CONTROLLED_PILOT_READY and PRODUCTION_CANDIDATE.VERIFY:- Run the app_pilot profile and paste the result in the report.- Confirm the production profile still fails as designed.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add app release gates and pilot profile```---Prompt 37 — P7.3: Evidence export, backup, and restore```PREAMBLEGOAL: An owner can export an evidence pack, and the app database can be backed up and restored.CONTEXT: The core already has scripts/export_evidence_pack.py and release/backup.py. Reuse both. Theexport is what a client's auditor sees, so it must include the app's own audit trail and the memorystore verification results, not just the core's.CHANGES:1. helix_codex_app/modules/admin/router.py — GET /app/admin/evidence/export (owner only), returning a   zip with the app audit trail, the node counts by kind, the memory store verification results, and   the release manifest.2. Extend the export to include a plain-text README inside the zip explaining what each file is, in   the same plain style as the rest of the repo.3. helix_codex_app/scripts/backup_app.py and restore_app.py — copy app.db and memory_stores/ to a   target, and restore from a backup, verifying the memory chain after a restore.4. Tests: the export zip contains the expected entries; a backup followed by a restore yields an   identical node count and a verifying memory chain.CONSTRAINTS:- The export must never include a password hash, a session token, or a raw secret.- Restore verifies the memory chain and fails loudly if it does not verify.VERIFY:- The export and backup tests pass.- Produce an export and open it; confirm the README explains each file.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: feat(app): add evidence export, backup, and restore```---Prompt 38 — P7.4: Package and deploy```PREAMBLEGOAL: One command brings the app up on a fresh machine.CONTEXT: The hybrid deployment decision means self-host first, cloud later. So the target is a singledocker compose up on the client's own hardware, serving the app, with the core available in-process.No Redis, no broker, no separate frontend host. This is the installer the client will actually run.CHANGES:1. infra/docker/docker-compose.app.yml — one service running helix-app, a volume for app.db and   memory_stores/, the environment variables the app needs, and a healthcheck hitting /app/healthz.2. infra/docker/Dockerfile.app — a slim Python 3.12 base, install the wheel with the web extra, run   as a non-root user, and start helix-app. Keep it small and readable.3. docs/release/app-setup-guide.md — the ten-minute install, written for a non-technical client   operator: what to install, what to run, how to reach the app, how to create the first owner   account, and how to back up. Plain language, numbered steps, no jargon.4. Add an app section to the existing operator runbook, or a new   docs/release/app-operator-runbook.md if the existing one is core-specific.5. Tests: a smoke test that the compose file parses and that the healthcheck path is correct.CONSTRAINTS:- No service other than the app. No database server, no cache, no queue.- The container runs as a non-root user and binds loopback by default; a public bind requires an  explicit environment change and a warning in the setup guide.- The setup guide must be readable by someone who is not an engineer.VERIFY:- docker compose -f infra/docker/docker-compose.app.yml up on a clean machine, then GET /app/healthz  returns 200. Time it and report the number.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: chore(app): add packaging, compose profile, and setup guide```---Prompt 39 — P7.5: Signoff — the full gate and the v1 record```PREAMBLEGOAL: Prove v1 is shippable, write the record, and close the pack.CONTEXT: A release is not a release until the gate has run and the record says what is and is notclaimed. This prompt produces that record. It claims nothing the tests did not show.CHANGES:1. Run the full suite and the app_pilot release profile. Record the exact numbers.2. Run ruff on the whole helix_codex_app/ tree and tests/helix_codex_app/.3. Regenerate the release manifest and confirm it reflects the current commit.4. Write docs/release/helix_codex_app_v1_signoff.md containing: the test count, the gate result and   its label, the ruff result, the list of what v1 does, the list of what v1 does not do (from the   master plan section 14), the known limitations, and the one-paragraph definition of shippable v1   from the master plan section 13.5. Update helix_codex_app/agents.md to mark P7 complete, with the final test count and SHA.6. Update CHANGELOG.md with one entry for the app's first release, in plain English.CONSTRAINTS:- Every number in the signoff must come from a command you ran in this session. Paste the command  output into the report.- If the gate does not pass, do not write a signoff claiming it did. Write the signoff that says what  failed and stop.VERIFY:- The app_pilot profile passes, or the signoff says exactly what failed.- The signoff lists no capability that a test does not cover.- Full suite >= 621 passed, 0 failed.- ruff clean.COMMIT: docs(app): record helix codex app v1 signoff```---12. Failure playbookA test fails. Fix the cause. Do not delete the test, do not mark it skip, do not loosen anassertion to make it pass. If the test itself was wrong, say so in the report and explain why the newassertion is the correct one.The baseline regresses. Stop. Revert to the last commit. Re-send the prompt with the failureoutput appended. A commit that lowers the test count is never acceptable, no matter how good thefeature is.A dependency is missing. The only dependency this pack permits is `python-multipart`. If youbelieve you need another, stop and report it rather than adding it. The answer is usually that thestandard library already does it.A prompt's premise turns out to be wrong — a file it names does not exist, a function it tells youto reuse has a different signature. Stop. Report the discrepancy with the exact evidence. Do notimprovise a redesign. The prompts are the plan; if the plan is wrong, the plan gets fixed first.The scope feels too big for one prompt. It probably is. Split it into two commits and say so. Twoclean commits beat one that half-works.You are tempted to skip the check-out protocol. Do not. The three governance files are the onlyreason a future agent can pick this up. A commit without them is a commit that loses information.---13. What "done" means for the whole packThe pack is complete when all of the following are true, and each one is checkable:The full suite passes at or above the baseline that Prompt 0 recorded, with zero failures.`ruff check helix_codex_app/ tests/helix_codex_app/` is clean.The `app_pilot` release profile passes and emits `CONTROLLED_PILOT_READY` or`PRODUCTION_CANDIDATE`, never a bare `PRODUCTION`.`docker compose -f infra/docker/docker-compose.app.yml up` brings the app up on a clean machine and`/app/healthz` returns 200.A coach on a phone can install the PWA, sign in, punch in, chat, read an SOP, finish a task, andsee the calendar.An employee account receives 403 on every cockpit and ops route.A user can create a memory proposal, see its evidence, get it approved by someone else, and roll itback.`docs/release/helix_codex_app_v1_signoff.md` exists and every number in it came from a command thatwas actually run.